{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1290182-soak-advantage-stamina","id":1290182,"title":"Soak Advantage > Stamina","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-02-20T18:32:31-08:00","id":1290182,"title":"Soak Advantage > Stamina","content":"This is very crunchy, but the Armour Advantage (which can be purchased by any cyborg character, and by some Nephilim/Laham) costs 1 freebie per dot.<br /> <br /> Every 1 point gives 1 soak against bashing and lethal, and every 2 points give 1 soak against aggravated. There is no limit listed in the book, so in theory you could just have 14/7 soak, not counting your stamina, assuming you invested every possible freebie point. On average that comes to 7 damage soaked against any bashing or lethal attack from this trait alone. Considering that Mage has zero dex penalty, stylish and comfortable clothing that gives 5 points of soak... you get where I'm going.<br /> <br /> If you go for the Nephilim merit you can get 7 points of Armour for 7 freebies (standard deal), plus you get all of the other benefits (and marginal downsides), with the caveat of having to take a Derangement.<br /> <br /> Stamina costs 5 freebies per dot.<br /> <br /> It can't soak lethal or aggravated at all in the default rules, without magickal assistance. Stamina is capped at 5 unless you're an Archmage or possess a merit that allows you to purchase stamina 5.<br /> <br /> Stamina has a significant advantage associated with it for mages who perform rituals. This has very little relevance in combat, but in downtimes can make a huge difference.<br /> <br /> Overall, it just looks like the Armour Advantage was very poorly balanced, which is a shame. I'm reminded of the Freak Legion powers which could stack to give Fomori soak which made them practically invulnerable, even compared with Methuselahs. Of course an ST can place an arbitrary cap on the Advantages, but it's kind of annoying that there wasn't a limit on some of them. Healing is another weird example, since it doesn't take that large an investment to become Wolverine, especially if we mix with Spark of Life.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 02-20-2019, 09:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-20T23:00:28-08:00","id":1290250,"title":"","content":"There's no limit listed in the book, but the chart only goes to 5.. so I wouldn't let it go higher than that. I also don't think it's intended to stack with worn armor.<br /> <br /> Armor isn't perfect. Armor can be aimed around and is obviously armor... wear magically boosted Stamina doesn't care where you get hit. A lot of magical attacks can completely ignore armor.<br /> <br /> There's also treating Stamina as just soak and ritual casting as a questionable choice. Stamina is an Attribute that can be rolled in plenty of circumstances. Armor's only good for avoiding damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-20T23:50:20-08:00","id":1290259,"title":"","content":"Yeah, there are quite a few things to consider here:<br /> <br /> <b>1) All the Freebie points!</b><br /> As you wrote, it goes to extremes when you invest most of your freebie points, yes - but that also technically leaves you with an Arete 1 Cyborg if you happen to be Awakened. And even when not accounting for any Awakedness you then have no freebies spent in an stats, abilities etc. to speak of, nor any other potentially tasty Enhancements or advantages<br /> <br /> <b>2) Your Armor is their Stamina</b><br /> Cyborg or not, your Stamina itself won't even let you soak Lethal. A Vampire or Shifter (in non-breed) gets lethal soak through their stamina alone, without any Dex penalty, and they don't even need to invest freebies. The shifter even get agg soak from this. And both parties have it easy to get their Stamina to 5+. And they could wear armor on top.<br /> <br /> <b>3) Armor is bulky</b><br /> Even though it's not outright stated, most armors described in the books (even when not giving dex penalties) still increase bulk. A Cyborg with 7+ armor certainly might not pass casual inspection if the ST rules it. Heck, when it comes to armor <i>without any running magickal effect</i>, even the Syndicates' prized Heimdall V Leadstopper vest (while giving no Dex Penalty) 'only' has an Armor rating of 4 - and there are only ten in existance.<br /> Note that the Nephilim are potentially exempt from this.<br /> <br /> <b>4) Stun! Stun! Stun!</b><br /> <i>People Always Forget The Stun Rule(TM)</i>. If your cyborg has a Stamina of 3, and somebody manages to get 3 damage through the soak, the cyborg is going to be stunned for the current *and* the next round - during which they also are easier to get hit again.<br /> <br /> <b>5) Stamina is more than soak</b><br /> As you already described, Stamina has more uses than soak - it describes your character's endurance for a lot of tasks (not just Rituals), it is a rating for the resistance against poisons, toxins, diseases, and is the only thing that helps you against non-physical attacks like Mind, or attacks that simply bypass armor.<br /> <br /> <b>6) Paradox in your face!</b><br /> Hypertech cybernetics are still, for the most part, vulgar Paradox-sponges in most areas of the WoD once it becomes obvious that Something Is Not Right Here. If you take a grenade/shotgun/whatever to the face and you 'happen' to mysteriously soak all that with invisible armor? Paradox incoming. Every time you get hit too hard.<br /> <br /> <b>7) MinMaxing always needs ST arbitration</b><br /> WoD is inherently utterly Min-Maxable. From creating combat grunts that couldn't think their way out of an elementary school math question but could arm-wrestle Arnold, to CharGenning Supernaturals that will maul an elder through sheer brute force, to twinky combinations of Merits or neglectable Flaws, the ST *always* needs to keep an eye out on balancing new characters. And this *always* has to do with most things not giving explicit rules for balancing. This Enhancement is just one of countless things, and in a way one of the less-worrysome ones. The StoryTeller System is supposed to be managed by the ST and plain logic. And especially the Nephilim Merit is explicitely on an 'If ST-allowed' basis.<br /> <br /> <b>8) You are expensive!</b><br /> Technically part of the ST-arbitration spiel, but a Cyborg *that* insanely armored is super rare - and potentially super expensive to create. Which means you owe somebody, big time. And that somebody is also very likely the Syndicate, which means you were made for a purpose: Only get used for specific missions where that armor is actually needed. Enjoy being tossed in the middle of the proverbial shitstorm, each time, every time. It's what you got made for.<br /> <br /> <b>9) Speaking of (non-)MinMaxing...</b><br /> Let's WhiteRoom this a bit:<br /> <br /> * <i>Starting character forces Mage</i>. Forces 3, Prime 2. Avatar 3. 3 Arete.<br /> This isn't very MinMax'd at all, is it? Except the Mage spends a Willpower, drops in 3 Quintessence, and then tosses a fireball at difficulty 4. On average they will get probably 3 successes, potentially 4. With forces that just became 8-10 aggravated damage as a result, of which the cyborg will soak about 3 on average. After spending 15 freebies on their armor. That's about 5 to 8 aggravated left. Even if the Cyborg does not die, they are definitely stunned and toasty.<br /> <br /> * <i>Starting character Mind mage</i>.<br /> What armor?<br /> <br /> * <i>Starting character Life mage</i>.<br /> What armor?<br /> <br /> * Starting character Life/Matter mage<br /> Dear god<br /> <br /> * <i>Werewolf</i><br /> Your 15-freebies cyborg has 7 soak against aggravated? The werewolf gets at least 7 die of aggravated damage to dish out - not counting any hit-roll bonuses. They also do this several times in a single round.<br /> <br /> * <i>Protean Vampire</i><br /> Potentially like the Werewolf, really. The Gangrel don't like your shenanigans.<br /> <br /> * <i>Potence Vampire</i><br /> These guys will actually have it quiiiiiiiite a bit harder against the 14-dice lethal soak, of all the things listed. These are the kinds of things the armor actually bulkwarks against. Brute, plain usual force.<br /> <br /> * <i>Dominate/Presence/Dementation Vampire</i><br /> What armor?<br /> <br /> Non of the above are really MinMax'd to speak of in those scenarios.<br /> It's worth remembering that your max'd out armor Cyborg <i>has an Arete and Spheres of 1, if they're even awakened</i>.<br /> <br /> -------<br /> <br /> So yeah, at first the gut reaction might be that the cost of 1 point per Lethal soak or 2 per Aggravated soak is unbalanced, but if you consider all the other Pros and Cons, all the freebies spent, the enemies the cyborg might face and the ever-present duty of ST arbitration during character generation, I am really not worried or non-okay with the rule as it is written right now.<br /> <br /> The only thing your ton of armor *really* protects you against, is mundane things (and those mundane things have a looooot more to worry about in the WoD...) and supernaturals that fall into the unlucky category of <i>&quot;really not statted against that&quot;</i> or <i>'&quot;brute non-aggravated force, but nooot really brute non-aggravated force enough&quot;.</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> .","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 02-21-2019, 04:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-21T10:56:30-08:00","id":1290431,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1290182-soak-advantage-stamina?p=1290250#post1290250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's no limit listed in the book, but the chart only goes to 5.. so I wouldn't let it go higher than that. I also don't think it's intended to stack with worn armor.<br /> <br /> Armor isn't perfect. Armor can be aimed around and is obviously armor... wear magically boosted Stamina doesn't care where you get hit. A lot of magical attacks can completely ignore armor.<br /> <br /> There's also treating Stamina as just soak and ritual casting as a questionable choice. Stamina is an Attribute that can be rolled in plenty of circumstances. Armor's only good for avoiding damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>If the Armour rating is a bullet proof vest then naturally that won't help you soak damage inflicted by magick in most cases. However if the armour is bound into your pattern with Matter/Life, as all serious Mage cybernetics seem to be, then it should apply even to internal damage. The &quot;armour&quot; is literally your body being able to soak more damage, it's not just having a resilient mesh under your skin, it's more holistic. At least that's how I'm interpreting it since the book doesn't tell us (I believe). I'd even argue that since it's part of your pattern it should transition into Ephemera when you enter the Umbra, and protect you there.<br /> <br /> Furthermore there's nothing to suggest a cap on any Advantage really, is there? A cap of 10 might be sensible, since every 2 points gives 1 soak against aggravated and that means capping at 5 is annoying.<br /> <br /> As for Advantage Armour not stacking with worn Armour... why? Both in terms of crunch and fluff. A cyborg wearing a bulletproof vest is a cyborg wearing a bulletproof vest. If you're willing to invest freebies into the former and lose dexterity to the latter, go ahead. Fluff-wise it makes perfect sense that they stack (unless the attack is internal or bypasses the vest).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 02-21-2019, 02:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-21T11:25:18-08:00","id":1290444,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1290182-soak-advantage-stamina?p=1290259#post1290259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, there are quite a few things to consider here:<br /> <br /> <b>1) All the Freebie points!</b><br /> As you wrote, it goes to extremes when you invest most of your freebie points, yes - but that also technically leaves you with an Arete 1 Cyborg if you happen to be Awakened. And even when not accounting for any Awakedness you then have no freebies spent in an stats, abilities etc. to speak of, nor any other potentially tasty Enhancements or advantages<br /> <br /> <b>2) Your Armor is their Stamina</b><br /> Cyborg or not, your Stamina itself won't even let you soak Lethal. A Vampire or Shifter (in non-breed) gets lethal soak through their stamina alone, without any Dex penalty, and they don't even need to invest freebies. The shifter even get agg soak from this. And both parties have it easy to get their Stamina to 5+. And they could wear armor on top.<br /> <br /> <b>3) Armor is bulky</b><br /> Even though it's not outright stated, most armors described in the books (even when not giving dex penalties) still increase bulk. A Cyborg with 7+ armor certainly might not pass casual inspection if the ST rules it. Heck, when it comes to armor <i>without any running magickal effect</i>, even the Syndicates' prized Heimdall V Leadstopper vest (while giving no Dex Penalty) 'only' has an Armor rating of 4 - and there are only ten in existance.<br /> Note that the Nephilim are potentially exempt from this.<br /> <br /> <b>4) Stun! Stun! Stun!</b><br /> <i>People Always Forget The Stun Rule(TM)</i>. If your cyborg has a Stamina of 3, and somebody manages to get 3 damage through the soak, the cyborg is going to be stunned for the current *and* the next round - during which they also are easier to get hit again.<br /> <br /> <b>5) Stamina is more than soak</b><br /> As you already described, Stamina has more uses than soak - it describes your character's endurance for a lot of tasks (not just Rituals), it is a rating for the resistance against poisons, toxins, diseases, and is the only thing that helps you against non-physical attacks like Mind, or attacks that simply bypass armor.<br /> <br /> <b>6) Paradox in your face!</b><br /> Hypertech cybernetics are still, for the most part, vulgar Paradox-sponges in most areas of the WoD once it becomes obvious that Something Is Not Right Here. If you take a grenade/shotgun/whatever to the face and you 'happen' to mysteriously soak all that with invisible armor? Paradox incoming. Every time you get hit too hard.<br /> <br /> <b>7) MinMaxing always needs ST arbitration</b><br /> WoD is inherently utterly Min-Maxable. From creating combat grunts that couldn't think their way out of an elementary school math question but could arm-wrestle Arnold, to CharGenning Supernaturals that will maul an elder through sheer brute force, to twinky combinations of Merits or neglectable Flaws, the ST *always* needs to keep an eye out on balancing new characters. And this *always* has to do with most things not giving explicit rules for balancing. This Enhancement is just one of countless things, and in a way one of the less-worrysome ones. The StoryTeller System is supposed to be managed by the ST and plain logic. And especially the Nephilim Merit is explicitely on an 'If ST-allowed' basis.<br /> <br /> <b>8) You are expensive!</b><br /> Technically part of the ST-arbitration spiel, but a Cyborg *that* insanely armored is super rare - and potentially super expensive to create. Which means you owe somebody, big time. And that somebody is also very likely the Syndicate, which means you were made for a purpose: Only get used for specific missions where that armor is actually needed. Enjoy being tossed in the middle of the proverbial shitstorm, each time, every time. It's what you got made for.<br /> <br /> <b>9) Speaking of (non-)MinMaxing...</b><br /> Let's WhiteRoom this a bit:<br /> <br /> * <i>Starting character forces Mage</i>. Forces 3, Prime 2. Avatar 3. 3 Arete.<br /> This isn't very MinMax'd at all, is it? Except the Mage spends a Willpower, drops in 3 Quintessence, and then tosses a fireball at difficulty 4. On average they will get probably 3 successes, potentially 4. With forces that just became 8-10 aggravated damage as a result, of which the cyborg will soak about 3 on average. After spending 15 freebies on their armor. That's about 5 to 8 aggravated left. Even if the Cyborg does not die, they are definitely stunned and toasty.<br /> <br /> * <i>Starting character Mind mage</i>.<br /> What armor?<br /> <br /> * <i>Starting character Life mage</i>.<br /> What armor?<br /> <br /> * Starting character Life/Matter mage<br /> Dear god<br /> <br /> * <i>Werewolf</i><br /> Your 15-freebies cyborg has 7 soak against aggravated? The werewolf gets at least 7 die of aggravated damage to dish out - not counting any hit-roll bonuses. They also do this several times in a single round.<br /> <br /> * <i>Protean Vampire</i><br /> Potentially like the Werewolf, really. The Gangrel don't like your shenanigans.<br /> <br /> * <i>Potence Vampire</i><br /> These guys will actually have it quiiiiiiiite a bit harder against the 14-dice lethal soak, of all the things listed. These are the kinds of things the armor actually bulkwarks against. Brute, plain usual force.<br /> <br /> * <i>Dominate/Presence/Dementation Vampire</i><br /> What armor?<br /> <br /> Non of the above are really MinMax'd to speak of in those scenarios.<br /> It's worth remembering that your max'd out armor Cyborg <i>has an Arete and Spheres of 1, if they're even awakened</i>.<br /> <br /> -------<br /> <br /> So yeah, at first the gut reaction might be that the cost of 1 point per Lethal soak or 2 per Aggravated soak is unbalanced, but if you consider all the other Pros and Cons, all the freebies spent, the enemies the cyborg might face and the ever-present duty of ST arbitration during character generation, I am really not worried or non-okay with the rule as it is written right now.<br /> <br /> The only thing your ton of armor *really* protects you against, is mundane things (and those mundane things have a looooot more to worry about in the WoD...) and supernaturals that fall into the unlucky category of <i>&quot;really not statted against that&quot;</i> or <i>'&quot;brute non-aggravated force, but nooot really brute non-aggravated force enough&quot;.</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>This is really well written! I disagree on a few things though.<br /> <br /> 1) Agreed, it's a slightly absurd min-max to be sure.<br /> <br /> 2) That's part of my reason for making this post; Stamina is very weak for Mages compared with Vampires and Werewolves, unless the ST uses cinematic damage.<br /> <br /> 3) None of the crunch of the Advantages section mentions the Armour Advantage being conspicuous. You're probably confusing them with Enhancements; extensive Enhancements can turn you into a veritable metal-head. Remember that armour made with Matter magick can be -0 to Dexterity; that's the sort of armour you're filling a cyborg with in the 21st century. Hit Marks that weigh a tonne are almost phased out.<br /> <br /> 4) Who's getting damage past 14 soak though? The stun rule could be crippling yes... but honestly this sort of concept is going to have Physical as primary, it's poured all its freebies into Armour after all, we're not going for any beauty pageants of spelling bees ;-)<br /> <br /> 5) Agreed, stamina does more than soak.<br /> <br /> 6) There is no Paradox associated with Advantages. Paradox is associated with Enhancements, if you take the true &quot;cyborg&quot; option rather than going for the other options which have other flaws. If you're a Cyborg with Armour 14 it just means your pattern can soak that much damage almost naturally. People will be shocked and awed but it's not Paradoxical in the same way that being under the protection of Fortitude isn't Paradoxical. RAW, and IMO RAI, even having angel's wings and glowing red night vision eyes won't cause Paradox, in the same way that knowing Sorcery doesn't cause paradox. Some of the peripheral benefits and drawbacks of being a mage like Supernatural Merits and Flaws are just &quot;part of reality&quot; essentially.<br /> <br /> 7) Oh absolutely, I only let people have things like Nephilim if they either ask for it with a fitting concept, or if they don't ask for it at all but it perfectly fits the character. And I'd never allow 14 in armour... but would I allow 10? 8? I don't know. It would be useful if the book told me.<br /> <br /> 8) Much cheaper than Primium, or filling you with energy weapons surely? Advantages is essentially being filled with Trinkets, while Enhancements is being filled with Wonders... by and large. And yes you'd be pointed in the direction of missions where armour was extremely valuable, but that goes for any employee.<br /> <br /> 9) A lot of this is assuming that most forms of damage just circumvent the Armour advantage. It says you can soak &quot;Aggravated Damage&quot;, for example. Not claws and fangs, not aggravated weapons... Aggravated damage. This leaves what that means at ST caveat. I think it's fair to interpret this as meaning that a forces Fireball gets soaked, electrification gets soaked, an attempt to melt your skull to fry your brain gets soaked, an attempt to unravel your pattern gets soaked... your Life pattern is just heavily reinforced with Matter that makes it stronger.<br /> <br /> Mind control, unsoakable damage, combat blender werewolves etc can override this naturally, but that's the problem with a white room situation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 02-21-2019, 02:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-21T12:41:26-08:00","id":1290494,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1290182-soak-advantage-stamina?p=1290444#post1290444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">9) A lot of this is assuming that most forms of damage just circumvent the Armour advantage. It says you can soak &quot;Aggravated Damage&quot;, for example. Not claws and fangs, not aggravated weapons... Aggravated damage. This leaves what that means at ST caveat. I think it's fair to interpret this as meaning that a forces Fireball gets soaked, electrification gets soaked, an attempt to melt your skull to fry your brain gets soaked, an attempt to unravel your pattern gets soaked... your Life pattern is just heavily reinforced with Matter that makes it stronger.<br /> <br /> Mind control, unsoakable damage, combat blender werewolves etc can override this naturally, but that's the problem with a white room situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's laaaate here, so I'll for now reply to this...and I just peeked at the Advantages you were referring to (I really confused them in parts with Enhancements, thanks for the heads up!) and noticed that the advantages bit from M20 page 658 (I presume?) mentions that more details will be given in Gods &amp; Monsters.<br /> <br /> I have the preview PDF of Gods &amp; Monsters and found the entry! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Lemme quote:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Armor (variable)<br /> Thanks to thick hide, armored plates, warty skin or some<br /> other form of innate protection, your character gains extra soak<br /> dice, and can soak lethal damage as well. For double the usual<br /> cost \u2014 that is, two freebie points per point of protection \u2014 she can<br /> soak aggravated damage, too. This armor isn\u2019t invisible, though;<br /> in most cases, it\u2019s pretty obvious, and for every three points in<br /> protection, the character loses one point of Appearance. (This<br /> penalty does not apply to animals or beasts with natural armor<br /> \u2014 turtles, alligators, dragons, and so forth.) As an optional<br /> rule, the Storyteller might decide that an aimed blow (typically<br /> +3 difficulty, although the specific penalty depends upon the<br /> nature of the armor) can bypass that protection.<br /> The normal cost for Armor can be found on the chart; ar-<br /> mor that soaks aggravated damage costs double these amounts. </div> </div> </div>So yeah. It's physical, external armor, and more visible the more points you invest in it. So Mind attacks, life pattern rips etc. go right through, and it will get bulky and visible as I suspected. That certainly clears things up. It also makes it clear that you need to invest 2 freebies *per armor point* to make the armor also soak Aggravated, so the full 15-freebies agg armor would also 'only' soak 7 dice against Lethal.<br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> <br /> The more detailed chart in that book also gives some details about the example nature of the armor:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 1 pt. = One soak die (thick fur or hide)<br /> 2 pts. = Two soak dice (scales, ridges)<br /> 3 pts. = Three soak dice (chitin, shell)<br /> 4 pts. = Four soak dice (bone plating, dragon hide)<br /> 5 pts. = Five soak dice (metal plates, thick hide plus layers of fat and muscle) </div> </div> </div>The chart indeed only goes up to 5 even in that book, so I presume that is indeed the max you can get. Max of 10 freebie points invested (for agg armor) and -3 to appearance. Although I guess Apprearance always depends on the eye of the beholder - it's pretty much a stat for mundane, mortal sensitivities.<br /> <br /> <br /> .","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 02-21-2019, 04:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-21T12:47:23-08:00","id":1290496,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1290182-soak-advantage-stamina?p=1290494#post1290494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's laaaate here, so I'll for now reply to this...and I just peeked at the Advantages you were referring to (I really confused them in parts with Enhancements, thanks for the heads up!) and noticed that the advantages bit from M20 page 658 (I presume?) mentions that more details will be given in Gods &amp; Monsters.<br /> <br /> I have the preview PDF of Gods &amp; Monsters! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Lemme quote:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So yeah. It's physical, external armor, and more visible the more points you invest in it. So Mind attacks, life pattern rips etc. go right through, and it will get bulky and visible as I suspected. That certainly clears things up. It also makes it clear that you need to invest 2 freebies *per points* to make the armor also soak Aggravated, so the full 15-freebies agg armor would also 'only' soak 7 against Lethal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah! I don't have Gods and Monsters yet. It's good that they decided to alter the rules and balance it out. It does say &quot;some other form of innate protection&quot; though.<br /> <br /> The investment is more balanced since the double cost is applied. Glad to see they took a closer look in this book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-21T13:50:52-08:00","id":1290520,"title":"","content":"And there is something to the argument &quot;min maxing for what?&quot;. The true min-maxing to me are things that modify experience like Quick Learner or Sphere Adept/Natural. The latter encourages a player to pick a best sphere with no dots so they can minimize the in game cost and quickly recover the freebie points.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15603-phillycuriosity","name":"PhillyCuriosity","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15603_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-21T14:05:55-08:00","id":1290529,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PhillyCuriosity</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1290182-soak-advantage-stamina?p=1290520#post1290520\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And there is something to the argument &quot;min maxing for what?&quot;. The true min-maxing to me are things that modify experience like Quick Learner or Sphere Adept/Natural. The latter encourages a player to pick a best sphere with no dots so they can minimize the in game cost and quickly recover the freebie points.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those EXP save merits make very little sense to me. If you're a natural at a sphere, then you show that by buying a dot in it.<br /> <br /> Starting with 0 in a sphere you're a natural in is... what? You see what I mean.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}